{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "246",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s, weighing 109kg, with stage IV KRAS wild-type rectal adenocarcinoma diagnosed in November 2013. He had pulmonary and liver metastases. His treatment history included radiation therapy and multiple chemotherapy cycles. In December 2014, he was enrolled in trial E7208 at the Smilow Cancer Hospital at Yale-New Haven and was randomized to receive combination therapy every 2 weeks.\n\nIn early January 2015, the patient developed a single, well-defined, blanchable bright red plaque densely composed of telangiectasias on his lower right lateral shin, which grew to 4 cm. The lesion was not associated with pain or pruritus.\n\n{'Histopathological Analysis': {'Findings': 'Discrete clusters of small-caliber vessels were observed, with superficial, small lobules of small-caliber vessels. The epidermis was normal.', 'Images': [{'Title': 'Clinical and Histological Features of Vascular Lesion', 'Description': 'Figure 1A shows a single, well-demarcated, blanchable red plaque, measuring 4 cm in diameter. The plaque breaks up into smaller punctate macules at the periphery.'}, {'Title': 'Histological Analysis', 'Description': 'Figure 1B and C show superficial, small lobules of small-caliber vessels (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×4 and ×20)'}]}, 'Genetic Analysis': {'Method': \"Paired whole-exome sequencing of affected tissue and patient's blood\", 'Finding': 'KDR (VEGFR2) c.C2312G, p.T771R identified as the only somatic mutation'}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Lobular capillary hemangioma (pyogenic granuloma)\nB. Tufted angioma with somatic KDR mutation\nC. Acquired tufted angioma\nD. Microvenular hemangioma\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Cutaneous Vascular Lesion with Telangiectasias and Somatic KDR Mutation",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
